  >
    Why?
  </h1>

  <div
    style="
      margin-top: 18px;
      color: #888;
      text-align: center;
      font-style: italic;
    "
  >
    <div>For the boy to whom I made an impossible promise.</div>
    <div>I tried.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="poem" style="max-width: 740px; margin: 36px auto 0;">
FYC

I dreamt of a golden tower
Built by mine own hands
That lanced the clouds and
Stabbed through heaven
And drenched the cosmos with life.
I dreamt.
And so none bore witness
To the shine and the hum
And the meat of it all.

NLTW 

—Then I wake, 
And I walk, 
And I work, 
And I live; 
And it lives on its own 
And East dies again. 
I look toward that temporary immortal 
And I see virgin land 
Seeded with flint that speaks 
And words that burn. 
  
LAA 

​I turn my back 
to the gold-flecked throat of the morning 
And seek comfort in the dust at my feet. 
I walk and I walk and I walk 
But this stillness is a glutton; 
A quiet erosion more violent than the gale. 
So I give my weight to the edge of the world,
To the shine and the hum and the meat, 
Because going is a lesser death
Than staying.

BD

The first stretch is a forgery.
My feet fall into the ruts of a stranger
And I pray it is only me I follow, 
Some future version returning in defeat 
So the ruin might be mine alone.
But the road is a loom of many steps;
The dust is the skin of everyone; 
The air is the breath of a dead god. 

S 

The black dog was behind me. 
Now, it leads. 
It noses the path through a ruined town 
Where the crumbled masonry stands pale as bone
And the roads are choked like constricted veins. 
Nothing flows. 
Nothing shines but the shattered windows, 
Every glinting shard, 
My eyes. 

MC 

Sleep comes in pieces, 
The rest in between, 
For when I closed my eyes 
I saw the shape of the last door, 
Its outline black against a whiter black. 
Alas, to see the gallows 
Does not shorten the walk, 
Nor does the knowledge of the blade 
hasten the stroke. 

O (Pilot) 

Ahead, a grey stranger wreathed in smoke. 
I still. To learn. To listen. Because 
I thought I was alone. 
But in the slope of his shoulders 
I read a failed ascent, 
And in his milky eyes 
There flicker thin survivals of gold, 
of honey. 
He leans toward me, 
The smoke parting to reveal his face. 
His mouth opens—

